How the poinsettia took over Christmas

The story of America=E2=80=99s favorite holiday plant is rife with internat=
ional politics, spoiled trade secrets, and a toppled monopoly.

BY ZACHARY CROCKETT (https://x.com/zzcrockett?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaV=
TqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_=
ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 )

The poinsettia plant is a ubiquitous Christmas staple.

Every year, just after Thanksgiving, it emerges en masse at nurseries, big-=
box retailers, fundraisers, and holiday parties.

It=E2=80=99s one of the most popular plants in the world, with annual sales=
 of ~90m units and a global retail impact of nearly $1B.

But behind the beautiful, blood-red bracts of the poinsettia, there=E2=80=
=99s a story rife with geopolitics, patent wars, a dethroned monopoly, and =
complex supply chains.

How did this Mexican shrub become America=E2=80=99s best-selling holiday pl=
ant?

To find out, The Hustle talked to poinsettia growers, breeders, salespeople=
, and historians.

poinsettias_youtube_thumbnail (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DmjMNn7M5wb=
E&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzE=
lSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 )

Deep roots

Indigenous to Southern Mexico, the poinsettia (or cuetlaxochitl) was first =
used (https://www.canr.msu.edu/uploads/resources/pdfs/the_history_of_the_po=
insettia.pdf?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9h=
pMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 ) by 14th=
-century Nahua people for dye and medicinal purposes.

The plant=E2=80=99s brilliant red bracts =E2=80=94 which are leaves, not fl=
owers =E2=80=94 were so revered by the Aztec emperor Montezuma that thousan=
ds of them were transported to the high-altitude capital of Tenochtitlan ea=
ch winter.

After Spain colonized (https://www.history.com/topics/mexico/history-of-mex=
ico?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKl=
zElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577#:~:text=3DSpaniard=
%20Hern%C3%A1n%20Cort%C3%A9s%20arrived%20at%20Veracruz%20in%201519.&text=3D=
In%20May%201521%2C%20Cort%C3%A9s%20and,most%20of%20the%20indigenous%20popul=
ation .) Mexico, Franciscan monks dubbed the plant flor de Nochebuena (=E2=
=80=9CFlower of the Blessed Night=E2=80=9D) and began to showcase it in ann=
ual Christmas processions.

One of the first-known taxonomic illustrations of the poinsettia (Curtis=E2=
=80=99s Botanical Magazine v.63; 1836)

For the next few centuries, the plant was cultivated and celebrated in Mexi=
co =E2=80=94 yet it remained obscure to the rest of the world.

Then, along came Joel Poinsett.

Poinsett, a wealthy Southern Unionist and slave owner, was appointed as the=
 first US minister to Mexico (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joel_Roberts_Po=
insett?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1=
qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 ) .

While there, he failed in an attempt to purchase Texas and became (https://=
www.americanantiquarian.org/proceedings/44817295.pdf?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95Lgn=
S0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7=
LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 ) a persona non grata for meddling in the co=
untry=E2=80=99s internal affairs.

But on a trip to the Southern town of Taxco in 1828, just before he was he =
recalled, he encountered the flor de Nochebuena and was so struck by it tha=
t he shipped specimens back to the US.

The plant =E2=80=94 which eventually became known to Westerners as the =E2=
=80=9Cpoinsettia=E2=80=9D =E2=80=94 made its public American debut at a flo=
wer show in Philadelphia and quickly became the talk of the town.

In news reports at the time, it was described as (https://www.newspapers.co=
m/image/465985592/?terms=3D%22poinsettia%22&match=3D1&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95Lg=
nS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y=
7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 ) =E2=80=9Cthe showiest flower to yet be se=
en=E2=80=9D and praised for having =E2=80=9Cthe most brilliant scarlet [lea=
ves] ]ever produced in the vegetable or mineral kingdom.=E2=80=9D

By the end of the 19th century, nurserymen were growing and distributing po=
insettias for ~$0.25 ($8 today) at local markets.

A portrait of Joel Roberts Poinsett superimposed over a background of red p=
oinsettias. (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3Dkl1eJmw295g&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz=
-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBa=
trO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 )

Joel Roberts Poinsett (Library of Congress, Charles Fenderich; photo illust=
ration by The Hustle)

But several things worked against the poinsettia as a commercial entity.

=E2=80=9CPerhaps no other plant or flower we handle=E2=80=A6 is short-lived=
, wilts quicker, or is more disappointing to those who receive it,=E2=80=9D=
 wrote (https://agris.fao.org/agris-search/search.do?recordID=3DGB201440177=
3&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzE=
lSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 ) Fritz Bahr, an ear=
ly floriculturist.

The plant would only last 2-3 days =E2=80=94 and its weak disposition meant=
 that it couldn=E2=80=99t readily be transported en masse.

It would take an enterprising family with a knack for marketing to help the=
 poinsettia realize its full saleable potential.

The =E2=80=98poinsettia prince=E2=80=99 and the rise of a monopoly

In 1900, a German immigrant named Albert Ecke packed his bags for Fiji, whe=
re he intended to open a health spa.

En route, he made a pitstop in Los Angeles and chose to settle there instea=
d =E2=80=94 a decision that would change the trajectory of agricultural his=
tory.

Ecke and his family established a dairy farm and a fruit orchard before eve=
ntually selling cut flowers, including poinsettias. By 1909, the poinsettia=
s were selling so well that he made them the focus of his entire business.

His son, Paul Ecke, assumed the business in the 1920s and moved the operati=
on to Encinitas, 25 miles north of San Diego.

Ecke soon developed secret breeding techniques that vastly improved the dur=
ability and aesthetics of poinsettias.

In the wake of the Plant Patent Act of 1930 (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Plant_Patent_Act_of_1930?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9=
dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D3951675=
77 ) , which allowed breeders to protect their new cultivars, he registered=
 dozens of his creations, staving off competitors and copycats.

Paul Ecke Sr. with some of his harvest (The Paul Ecke Ranch, Inc. Business =
Papers and Family Records, via CSU San Marcos archives)

The specifics of Ecke=E2=80=99s prized breeding technique =E2=80=94 which h=
e acquired from an amateur German gardener =E2=80=94 were guarded with the =
intensity of the Coca-Cola recipe.

=E2=80=9CNobody at the ranch knew the secret,=E2=80=9D his grandson, Paul E=
cke III, later told the Los Angeles Times. =E2=80=9CMy grandfather, my dad,=
 and their breeder knew, and it was done at the breeder=E2=80=99s home so n=
obody could see.=E2=80=9D

The result was a fuller, hardier plant with more branches =E2=80=94 a produ=
ct equipped to endure the tribulations of consumerism.

At the same time, under the leadership of Ecke=E2=80=99s son, Paul Jr., the=
 company marketed the plant as the premier Christmas decoration, sending fr=
ee samples to women=E2=80=99s magazines and making the rounds on prime-time=
 TV programs like =E2=80=9CThe Tonight Show.=E2=80=9D

By the 1990s, the family was selling 500k+ potted plants, and more than 25m=
 cuttings =E2=80=94 baby sections used to produce new plants =E2=80=94 to o=
ther growers on a royalty basis.

At its peak, the company had a virtual monopoly on the US poinsettia market=
, maintaining a 90% market share and 150+ patents (https://patents.justia.c=
om/assignee/paul-ecke-ranch?page=3D16&_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1P=
KOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_h=
smi=3D395167577 ) .

=E2=80=9CThe Eckes of Southern California are to poinsettias what De Beers =
of South Africa is to diamonds,=E2=80=9D one reporter later proclaimed.

While competitors popped up, none could challenge the exposure, prominence,=
 and rich IP of Ecke Ranch.

That is, until the worst possible scenario came to fruition.

Top: Three generations of the Ecke family (left to right): Paul Ecke III, P=
aul Ecke Sr., and Paul Ecke Jr. (The Paul Ecke Ranch, Inc. Business Papers =
and Family Records, via CSU San Marcos archives); Bottom: Paul Ecke Ranch i=
n Encinitas (Tripadvisor)

In 1992, a graduate student named John Dole got his hands on an Ecke cuttin=
g and managed to reverse-engineer the company=E2=80=99s top-secret process =
=E2=80=94 a method that involved grafting together 2 poinsettia plants.

His published findings (https://journals.ashs.org/jashs/view/journals/jashs=
/117/6/article-p972.xml?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9d=
KxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D39516757=
7 ) completely upended the poinsettia industry.

=E2=80=9CYou don=E2=80=99t often see a situation in science where one paper=
 makes a serious impact on the market,=E2=80=9D Dole, now an associate dean=
 at North Carolina State University, told The Hustle. =E2=80=9CBut in this =
case, it really did.=E2=80=9D

Competition flooded in, sparking a =E2=80=9Cgolden age=E2=80=9D for poinset=
tias:

The industry=E2=80=99s main focus shifted from cultivation to breeding, and=
 dozens of new color variations popped up =E2=80=94 hues of white, yellow, =
neon green, and pink =E2=80=94 with names like =E2=80=9CPremium Picasso=E2=
=80=9D and =E2=80=9CMonet Twilight.=E2=80=9D

Big-box retailers also began to change the dynamic of the market.

=E2=80=9CIn the earlier years, poinsettias were mostly sold at independent =
garden centers and were thought of as an upscale plant,=E2=80=9D says Dole.=
 =E2=80=9CBut when the mass marketers started selling them, that perception=
 changed.=E2=80=9D

Home Depot, Lowes, and Walmart began purchasing vast quantities of poinsett=
ias and selling them as loss leaders for as little as $0.99 (https://thekra=
zycouponlady.com/2021/12/02/1-clearance-poinsettias-at-lowes?_hsenc=3Dp2ANq=
tz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVh=
BatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 ) each.

Zachary Crockett / The Hustle

These changes pressured growers to ruthlessly cut overhead expenses to comp=
ete, eventually ushering in an era of consolidation. Giant agriculture firm=
s snatched up smaller outfits and shifted production overseas.

After slowly watching its monopoly decline, Ecke =E2=80=94 the one-time kin=
g of poinsettias =E2=80=94 sold its business (https://www.sandiegouniontrib=
une.com/sdut-agriculture-ecke-ranch-sold-to-dutch-company-2012aug16-story.h=
tml?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKl=
zElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577#:~:text=3DEcke%20R=
anch%2C%20the%20iconic%20Encinitas,marketing%20manager%20for%20Ecke%20Ranch=
 .) to a Dutch conglomerate in 2012.

It marked the end of an era =E2=80=94 and the beginning of a new, highly co=
mplex, international pipeline.

The modern economics of poinsettias

Today, Ecke is owned by D=C3=BCmmen Orange, one of the largest plant breede=
rs in the world.

Rebecca Siemonsma, who worked at Ecke from 2004-2012 and now oversees D=C3=
=BCmmen=E2=80=99s poinsettia operations, tells The Hustle that the company =
sells around 50m of the plants each year =E2=80=94 more than half of the wo=
rld=E2=80=99s supply.

In other words, it=E2=80=99s likely that the poinsettias you see at your lo=
cal Home Depot come from D=C3=BCmmen.

The journey of a poinsettia plant looks something like so:

Zachary Crockett / The Hustle

According to Dole, the horticulture professor, soil regulations (https://ww=
w.aphis.usda.gov/aphis/ourfocus/planthealth/import-information/permits/plan=
t-pests/sa_soil?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5O=
g9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 ) prev=
ent the importation of potted poinsettias. Only cuttings are permitted =E2=
=80=94 so the final stages of growing still happen in the US at major green=
houses.

Cuttings only sell for ~$0.30 each, but large firms like D=C3=BCmmen make m=
oney by selling millions of them at a time.

=E2=80=9CPoinsettias are a low-margin, high-volume business,=E2=80=9D says =
Siemonsma. =E2=80=9CThey=E2=80=99ve become somewhat of an inexpensive commo=
dity in the horticulture industry. But on a unit basis, they=E2=80=99re sti=
ll near the top.=E2=80=9D

This can make the economics a bit tricky for growers.

For most growers, poinsettias aren=E2=80=99t a huge money maker; they=E2=80=
=99re usually thrown into the mix for several practical reasons:

- They=E2=80=99re the rare plant that can be grown during the late summer m=
onths, and they allow growers to mitigate some of their fixed overhead cost=
s during an otherwise stagnant season.
- They allow growers to keep labor employed year-round, rather than just du=
ring the busy spring months.

Average wholesale prices for poinsettias have remained stagnant for several=
 decades. Adjusted for inflation, they=E2=80=99ve declined considerably.

Zachary Crockett / The Hustle

But poinsettias have remained lucrative for some.

Wenke Greenhouses is one of the 30 largest greenhouse operations in the US,=
 with several million square feet of crops in Michigan and Georgia.

The company started growing poinsettias in the mid-=E2=80=9980s. Today, it =
produces ~200k annually, which are sold wholesale to Walmart, Lowes, church=
es, and fundraisers.

=E2=80=9CWe=E2=80=99ve had 2 great years,=E2=80=9D says Lorence Wenke, the =
company=E2=80=99s long-time owner, who recently passed the business on to h=
is daughter. =E2=80=9CPeople are staying indoors and plants are thriving ac=
ross the board.=E2=80=9D

Kevin Koeppler is the general manager at Sunbelt Greenhouses, a subsidiary =
of Wenke Greenhouses based in Douglas, Georgia, where the poinsettias are g=
rown.

In a large spreadsheet shared with The Hustle, he broke down what the poins=
ettia sector of the business looks like on the back end:

Zachary Crockett / The Hustle

Those are far better margins than most in the business =E2=80=94 but Koeppl=
er says there are a myriad of things that can go wrong along the way.

=E2=80=9CThere are always a few unexpected things that come up in agricultu=
re,=E2=80=9D he says. =E2=80=9COne pest infestation might set you back $60k=
 and wipe out most of your margins.=E2=80=9D

COVID and ongoing supply chain issues have also put a dent in Koeppler=E2=
=80=99s usual process.

For instance, he used to import coconut coir =E2=80=94 the fibrous coconut =
husks inside shells =E2=80=94 from Indonesia to mix into his soil. But in r=
ecent years, he=E2=80=99s had to ditch it because shipping costs have ballo=
oned from $2.1k to $21k per container.

A fresh crop of poinsettias at Sunbelt Greenhouses in Douglas, Georgia (Sun=
belt Greenhouses)

One faction who feels they=E2=80=99ve been left out to dry in this booming =
poinsettia market is Mexican growers.

Though Mexico sells ~$12m (https://fronterasdesk.org/content/1643990/poinse=
ttia-sales-struggle-bloom-mexico-during-pandemic?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7=
aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrr=
HT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577 ) worth of poinsettia cuttings to the US market =
every year, a century-old foreign soil restriction (https://gpnmag.com/news=
/mexico-eyes-us-poinsettia-sales/?_hsenc=3Dp2ANqtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3=
lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFVhBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=
=3D395167577 ) prevents the country from selling its own native plant in po=
tted form to US consumers. The Mexican Embassy has lobbied (https://www.des=
eret.com/2004/12/18/19867408/mexico-sees-red-over-poinsettias?_hsenc=3Dp2AN=
qtz-95LgnS0a7aaVTqseNm1PKOI3lL08A6sG9dKxMj8E5Og9hpMZse1qKlzElSS_nFz-UmEHZFV=
hBatrO7y7LSrrHT_ovRVg&_hsmi=3D395167577#ron-simmons-left-and-todd-kendall-p=
ackage-poinsettias-at-diamonds-greenhouse-in-springville-thursday-workers-t=
here-start-in-july-planting-8000-pots-of-the-mexican-plant ) to change thes=
e restrictions with no luck.

Some growers feel embittered that a plant with deep native roots is now pat=
ented and controlled by foreign entities: Today, across the border, poinset=
tismo is used as a derogatory term to describe a person who is arrogant and=
 intrusive.

But on US soil, the market =E2=80=94 and the name =E2=80=94 isn=E2=80=99t l=
ikely to change.

=E2=80=9CNothing says it=E2=80=99s Christmas time like a poinsettia,=E2=80=
=9D says Wenke. =E2=80=9CAnd I think it=E2=80=99ll be that way for a long t=
ime.=E2=80=9D

